    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d726">

                <pb facs="0740" n="728" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg728"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/10828</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by the Assistant
                        Chief of the Division of Near Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Alling</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-05-03">May 3, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Colonel Cox<anchor n="11" xml:id="d726-f1"/> telephoned today to say he thought
                    we might be interested in knowing that MID had just been informed by General von
                    Boetticher, the German Military Attaché, that German forces had occupied the
                    following islands in the Aegean Sea: Skyros, Myconas, Nikaria and Suda. Colonel
                    Cox said that MID had been able to identify the first three islands, but they
                    could not identify Suda. Upon investigation it appears likely that the island in
                    question is probably Syros, but this is not certain.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d726-f1" n="11" xml:id="d726-f1-content">
                <p>Lt Col. Walter Cox, Military Intelligence Division, War Department.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
